  "result": "**Rating: 3/10.** The conclusion is wrong, though your goal is the right one to be asking about.\n\n**Correction**\n- A language model does not remember earlier conversations on its own.\n- Each request is stateless. The model sees only the text sent in that request.\n- Chatbots appear to remember because the application re-sends the earlier messages with each new one.\n\n**Consequence for your assistant**\n- Last week's conversation is gone unless your code saves it.\n- Remembering a student's training status means your code has to:\n  - store it (for example, a JSON record per student),\n  - load it at the start of a session,\n  - put it in the model's context.\n\n**Analogy**\n- Think of a shop-floor tech who gets a fresh clipboard at every shift.\n- Nothing carries over unless someone writes it on the clipboard and hands it over.\n- The analogy breaks down because the model cannot flip back through old pages. It reads only what is on the clipboard now.\n\n**Check**\n- Your assistant must know whether a student completed laser-cutter training.\n- Which of these should hold that fact: the model, or your storage? Give one reason.\n\nThis is the next topic on your mission (context and memory). I can write a lesson on it with a worked example and practice problems if you want one.",
